### UI as a Function of State

React's core idea is simple: **UI = f(state)**. Your UI is a pure function of your data. When the data changes, React re-renders the affected parts of the UI automatically.

```jsx
// Traditional imperative approach (jQuery-style)
document.getElementById('count').textContent = count;
document.getElementById('btn').disabled = count >= 10;

// React declarative approach
function Counter({ count }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <p>{count}</p>
      <button disabled={count >= 10}>Increment</button>
    </div>
  );
}
// You describe *what* the UI should look like, not *how* to update it.
```

#### The Virtual DOM

React maintains a lightweight in-memory representation of the real DOM. When state changes:

1. React re-renders the component to a **new virtual DOM tree**.
2. It **diffs** the new tree against the previous one (reconciliation).
3. It applies only the **minimal set of real DOM changes** needed.

This is why React is fast — real DOM operations are expensive; object comparison is cheap.

---

### Components are the Building Blocks

A React component is a function that takes props and returns JSX.

```jsx
// Function component (modern standard)
function UserCard({ name, role, avatarUrl }) {
  return (
    <div className="card">
      <img src={avatarUrl} alt={name} />
      <h2>{name}</h2>
      <span>{role}</span>
    </div>
  );
}

// Usage
<UserCard name="Alice" role="Engineer" avatarUrl="/alice.jpg" />
```

#### JSX Rules

```jsx
// 1. Return a single root element (or Fragment)
return (
  <>
    <h1>Title</h1>
    <p>Paragraph</p>
  </>
);

// 2. Close all tags
<img src="..." />  // Not: <img src="...">

// 3. className, not class
<div className="container">

// 4. Expressions in curly braces
<p>{user.name.toUpperCase()}</p>
<button onClick={() => handleClick(id)}>Click</button>

// 5. Lists need a key prop
{items.map(item => (
  <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
))}
```

